---
import ProductLink from '../../components/ProductLink.astro';
import AffLink from '../../components/AffLink.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Newsletter from '../../components/Newsletter.astro';
import batteries from '../../data/batteries.json';
import costs from '../../data/costs.json';
import { priceLow } from '../../data/site.js';
const b = (n) => batteries.find(x => x.product === n);
const usd = (v) => '$' + v.toLocaleString('en-US', { maximumFractionDigits: 0 });
const row = (n) => { const x = b(n); return x ? { name:x.product, kwh:x.capacity_kwh.toFixed(2), price:usd(priceLow(x.price)), src:x.source_url } : null; };
const t1 = ['Anker SOLIX C1000X','Jackery Explorer 1000 v2'].map(row).filter(Boolean);
const t2 = ['Jackery Explorer 2000 v2','Bluetti Elite 200 v2'].map(row).filter(Boolean);
const t3 = ['EcoFlow DELTA Pro 3','Jackery Explorer 5000 Plus','EcoFlow DELTA Pro Ultra'].map(row).filter(Boolean);
const installed = costs.find(c => c.item.startsWith('Installed home battery'));
const transfer = costs.find(c => c.item.startsWith('Plug-in transfer'));
const faq = [
["Is there a specific threat behind the emergency prep push?", "No. The UK government, the officials behind the European campaigns, and the Pentagon all describe their guidance as precautionary planning for storms, cyberattacks, and other disruptions, not a response to a specific threat."],
["What's the cheapest useful backup power for an outage?", "A roughly 1 kWh portable power station (about $500–$509 as of November 7, 2026 in our price table) keeps phones, Wi-Fi, and LED lights going for about 2 days. It won't run a refrigerator for long."],
["Should I buy a standby generator before winter?", "If you want one, get quotes now. A Pittsburgh installer told WESA on Oct. 27, 2026 that a call in November for a generator before Christmas is 'not going to happen' because demand is so high."]
];
---
<Base title="Why Emergency Prep Is Surging (Nov 2026) | HomeBackupCost" description="Government 'be ready' campaigns, the Pentagon's FORTRESS grid plan, and early storms have put emergency prep everywhere. What it means for home backup power, at every budget." updated="2026-11-02" h1Name="Why emergency prep is surging" schema={[{ '@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'FAQPage', mainEntity: faq.map(([q,a]) => ({ '@type':'Question', name:q, acceptedAnswer:{ '@type':'Answer', text:a } })) }]}>
<h1>Why everyone is suddenly talking about emergency prep, and what it means for your home's power</h1>
<p class="lede">If your feed is full of "72-hour kits" and generators right now, you're not imagining it. Here's what's driving it, checked against the original sources, and a calm plan at four budgets. Facts as of November 2, 2026.</p>

<h2>What's actually going on</h2>
<p><strong>1. Governments are telling households to keep a few days of supplies.</strong> The UK launched a "Be Ready" campaign in October 2026 asking households to prepare for up to three days of disruption from power cuts, severe weather, and cyberattacks. Retailers reported a jump in sales afterward: Toolstation said generator sales rose 172% in the campaign's launch week, and Maplin reported a 106% rise in power banks (<a href="https://londonlovesbusiness.com/generator-sales-surge-172-as-uk-households-respond-to-emergency-warning/">London Loves Business, Oct. 28, 2026</a>). Those are single-retailer figures, not national totals. Similar guidance from other European governments is circulating widely online.</p>
<p><strong>2. The Pentagon's FORTRESS plan put "off-grid" in the headlines.</strong> Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth said on Sept. 30 that every major U.S. military installation will "generate its own power, independent of the civilian grid" (<a href="https://defensescoop.com/2026/10/07/pentagon-memo-operationalize-fortress-america-program-office/">DefenseScoop</a>). A widely shared 90-day self-sufficiency figure comes from an Oct. 25 Fox News report; Snopes rated it "mostly true" but says the Pentagon hasn't explicitly confirmed it (<a href="https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/pentagon-fortress-initiative-90-days/">Snopes, Oct. 26, 2026</a>). <a href="/news/pentagon-fortress-grid-plan-homeowners/">What FORTRESS means for homeowners →</a></p>
<p><strong>3. Outages at home are getting longer, and generator installers are busy.</strong> In Pennsylvania, 52 of 55 reportable outages in 2025 were weather-related, and about 1.7 million customers were hit by major events, up from about 696,000 in 2024 (Pennsylvania PUC figures reported by <a href="https://www.wesanews.org/environment-energy/2026-10-27/generators-pittsburgh-power-outage-weather-utility">90.5 WESA, Oct. 27, 2026</a>). Duquesne Light customers averaged about 3 hours of outage time in 2024, up from about 70 minutes a decade earlier. A local installer told WESA demand is "booming" and the industry is seeing double-digit growth.</p>
<p><strong>4. Storm season is shifting into winter.</strong> Winter storms, ice, and wet snow bring down trees and lines. NERC, which oversees North American grid reliability, is scheduled to release its 2026–2027 Winter Reliability Assessment on Nov. 18 (<a href="https://www.nerc.com/globalassets/who-we-are/standing-committees/rstc/ras/2026_2027_wra_schedule.pdf">NERC schedule</a>). We'll update this story when it's out.</p>
<p>None of the officials involved point to a specific threat. Their message is the same one Ready.gov has given for years: have enough food, water, and supplies to get by for several days (<a href="https://www.ready.gov/kit">Ready.gov</a>). Power is the part most people haven't planned for.</p>

<h2>What to actually buy or do, by budget</h2>
<p class="src">Prices are the lowest listed price in our <a href="/portable-power-stations/">power station table</a>, checked November 7, 2026. Prices change often.</p>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>$0: plan first</h3><p>Work out what you actually need to keep running. Phones, Wi-Fi, and six LED bulbs take about 3.1 kWh over 3 days; adding a refrigerator brings it to about 10.9 kWh (<a href="/72-hour-power-outage-plan/">72-hour plan</a>). Keep phones and power banks charged when storms are forecast.</p><p><a href="/calculator/">Run your numbers in the calculator →</a></p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>About $500: stay connected</h3><ul>{t1.map(x => <li><ProductLink product={x.name} button={false} />: {x.kwh} kWh, {x.price} <a class="src" href={x.src}>source</a><br/><AffLink product={x.name} /></li>)}</ul><p>Covers phones, Wi-Fi, and lights for about 2 days. Not enough for a fridge for long.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>About $900: keep food cold for a day</h3><ul>{t2.map(x => <li><ProductLink product={x.name} button={false} />: {x.kwh} kWh, {x.price} <a class="src" href={x.src}>source</a><br/><AffLink product={x.name} /></li>)}</ul><p>About 2 kWh. A 2.04 kWh station covers the "stay connected" level for about 2 days, or runs a fridge part-time. Add solar to stretch it. <a href="/solar-backup/">Solar backup →</a></p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>$2,800–$4,200: most of a house's essentials</h3><ul>{t3.map(x => <li><ProductLink product={x.name} button={false} />: {x.kwh} kWh, {x.price} <a class="src" href={x.src}>source</a><br/><AffLink product={x.name} /></li>)}</ul><p>Enough for fridge, lights, Wi-Fi, and a furnace blower for a day or more. Plug-in transfer hardware ({transfer?.range}) lets it feed home circuits.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>$10,000+: whole-home</h3><p>An installed ~13.5 kWh home battery runs {installed?.range} (<a href={installed?.source}>EnergySage</a>). Standby generators also live here. If you want one before winter, start quotes now; installers are booked up. <a href="/whole-home-battery-cost/">Battery costs →</a> · <a href="/standby-generator-installation-cost/">Standby generator costs →</a></p></div>
</div>

<h2>What not to do</h2>
<ul>
<li>Don't panic-buy the biggest unit you can find. Size it to what you need with the <a href="/calculator/">calculator</a> or the <a href="/what-size-battery-generator/">sizing guide</a>.</li>
<li>Never run a gas generator indoors or in a garage. Keep it at least 20 feet from windows and doors (<a href="https://www.ready.gov/power-outages">Ready.gov</a>). <a href="/generator-safety/">Generator safety →</a></li>
<li>Don't plug a generator into a dryer outlet ("backfeeding"). It can electrocute line workers. Use a transfer switch or interlock kit. <a href="/transfer-switch-vs-interlock-kit/">Transfer switch vs interlock →</a></li>
</ul>
